#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Course Keeper's desktop card, driven by the desktop stand-in (control start --surface
desktop) with scripted model replies. The stand-in runs the desktop app's own pinned Claude
Code (or the binary given by --cli) and plays the app's role over the app's stream-json
protocol. It never starts the desktop app.

Four runs. The main run starts with --attach-later. It proves that the plugin loads under
the CLI, that a person's turn before ui_attach records "no surface is attached", the
card's parts in the tree that the CLI returns for ui_render at the app's maxRows 12, each
button through ui_press (Details, Tell the agent, False alarm, the close Button), the
notification call without CLAUDE_CODE_NOTIFICATIONS_HOST, the ui_toast frame for a new
standing instruction, an instruction alert's headline, off, check and on, a short learn,
and that the plugin's haiku call holds the person's words without the reminder when the
frame carries a leading system-reminder block. A fresh run without the doctor proves that
a frame without client_platform is no person and that the app's envelope is one. A run
with --max-rows below CARD_ROWS proves the short-band order. A run with
--notifications-host and --columns 1 proves the ui_notify frame and the card's Buttons at the app's first measure.

  journeys/desktop [--cli PATH]

  --cli PATH   the CLI that each run's `control start --surface desktop` runs (default: the
               desktop app's newest verified build)

What this proves: the tree the CLI returns, the frames it sends and what the plugin stores,
under the stand-in. It does not prove how the app paints the card (layout, line cuts,
colours, the close mark), its keyboard handling or focus, its in-app toast or native banner,
or the scroll that Details asks for. A scripted reply proves how the plugin handles a reply,
not what a real model detects.
"""
